  "draft_notes": {
    "explanation": "This family needs no D4-specific constants. The legend markers are spaced along one line. Copy i of the template goes at block i along the same axis, in the same spatial direction as marker i. Every D4 transform (rotation or reflection) acts identically on input and output, so this spatial relation is preserved in the visible coordinates. A row of markers gives a left-to-right strip, and the marker with the smaller column gets the leftmost block. A column of markers gives a top-to-bottom stack, and the marker with the smaller row gets the topmost block. The template is cropped in visible coordinates, so it already carries the transformed shape. Only the colour roles are specialised. Background is palette[0], and the template colour is palette[1] (original blue). Each stamp fills template cells with the marker's colour and every other cell with the background. This is the visible version of icolor*jcolor, where the pattern's 0 cells become background. The orientation is chosen by whether the markers share a row or a column. There are always at least 2 markers, spaced 2 apart, so this is unambiguous. Markers are any cell that is neither background nor template colour. A marker colour may coincide with another marker's colour, and this is handled.",
    "limitations": "The tile is the bounding box of the template-colour cells, as in the archived solver. If a generated sprite ever failed to span its full 3x3 cell in some dimension, the true 3x3 tile offset could not be recovered from the input, and the output would be smaller than 3 per copy. This assumes diagonally_connected_sprite yields sprites that fill their 3x3 bounding box, which is consistent with all fixtures. The fixtures are consistent with the uniform rule, and no contradictions were found. I have not run any tests."
  }
